 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d001: excitatory cascade (k=6)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 11.7 and radius 1.52 with 6 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.30) and a 4-dim charge; 5 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 19 couplings (21% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 1.00 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.56 bonds per particle, largest molecule 3.5 particles, 16.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.114 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.114  random bonds 0.112\n  xor      0.610 vs ESN 0.529\u00b10.011  [beat]  no bonds 0.637  random bonds 0.627\n  narma10  0.277 vs ESN 0.841\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.288  random bonds 0.290\n  latch    0.800 vs ESN 0.679\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.804  random bonds 0.811\n  mnist14  0.490 vs ESN 0.816\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.400  random bonds 0.423\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
